00:35:04,000 --> 00:35:09,000
I can't promise to always do that, but I do try to give you the math when you are at your most alert.

268
00:35:09,000 --> 00:35:12,000
All right. Now we're zooming.

269
00:35:12,000 --> 00:35:16,000
All right. Now, some relationships between these concepts.

270
00:35:16,000 --> 00:35:21,000
First of all, so every calorie-exhibition situation is Fredo-efficient.

271
00:35:21,000 --> 00:35:24,000
Every calorie-exhibition situation is Fredo-efficient.

272
00:35:24,000 --> 00:35:29,000
But most Fredo-efficient situations are not calorie-exhibition.

273
00:35:29,000 --> 00:35:31,000
And how can we see this?

274
00:35:31,000 --> 00:35:33,000
Let's go back to that watch example.

275
00:35:33,000 --> 00:35:37,000
I suppose that you value your watch at $20.

276
00:35:37,000 --> 00:35:39,000
I value your watch at $30.

277
00:35:39,000 --> 00:35:46,000
Then the people that will ask you the time over the course of your ownership of the watch value that at a cumulative amount of 10 cents,

278
00:35:46,000 --> 00:35:48,000
and same for the people that will ask me.

279
00:35:48,000 --> 00:35:51,000
Of course, my strangers are different from your strangers.

280
00:35:51,000 --> 00:35:57,000
All right. Now, if I have the watch, is the situation Fredo-efficient?

281
00:35:57,000 --> 00:36:01,000
Sure. Right? And well, you know, yeah, I'll let them know so.

282
00:36:01,000 --> 00:36:04,000
Now, is it calorie-exhibition?

283
00:36:04,000 --> 00:36:07,000
And assume we're the only two people who can own this watch.

284
00:36:07,000 --> 00:36:10,000
Well, so if I have the watch, what's the social value of the ownership?

285
00:36:10,000 --> 00:36:14,000
$30.10. If you have it, it's $20.10.

286
00:36:14,000 --> 00:36:21,000
Now, why is it that if, you know, that as long as, let's see, you know,

287
00:36:21,000 --> 00:36:26,000
how would it be the problem if, say, if you owned it, right?

288
00:36:26,000 --> 00:36:34,000
So if you owned it, and then at least, for instance, I could go and, you know,

289
00:36:34,000 --> 00:36:38,000
pay you the money and then buy people off, of course, in the real world, that's going to be a problem, right?

290
00:36:38,000 --> 00:36:43,000
But if we are at the maximum value of $30.10, there's no surplus to divide.

291
00:36:43,000 --> 00:36:47,000
So if you already maxed out the surplus, there's no way you can get a Fredo-improvement out of that,

292
00:36:47,000 --> 00:36:51,000
even in a very high-life theoretical setting, because if there's no,

293
00:36:51,000 --> 00:36:57,000
if everything is already owned by the highest value ever, then how can the value go up again?

294
00:36:57,000 --> 00:36:59,000
You can't. Right?

295
00:36:59,000 --> 00:37:05,000
If every item is already possessed by whoever values it the most, there's no longer any room for a Fredo-improvement,

296
00:37:05,000 --> 00:37:10,000
even if other things are, other conditions are suspicious.

297
00:37:10,000 --> 00:37:15,000
Now, so anyway, if you have the loss, the situation is still Fredo-efficient, again, in the real world,

298
00:37:15,000 --> 00:37:19,000
because there's no way of compensating those people who have asked you the time, right?

299
00:37:19,000 --> 00:37:23,000
But it's not Calgary-exficient. Calgary-efficiency would be if I had it,

300
00:37:23,000 --> 00:37:28,000
then society values of $30.10 combined, but of course still Fredo-efficient for you to have it,

301
00:37:28,000 --> 00:37:32,000
because we don't have any compensation scheme that is workable.

302
00:37:32,000 --> 00:37:38,000
Right. Now, every Fredo-improvement, every time we actually have a Fredo-improvement,

303
00:37:38,000 --> 00:37:40,000
we also have a Calgary-ex-improvement automatically.

