you just did an interpersonal utility comparison, didn't you?

38
01:22:58,000 --> 01:23:01,000
What would your life be like if you just stopped ever doing that again?

39
01:23:01,000 --> 01:23:04,000
And suddenly, you know, who's happier? Who's happiness in this room?

40
01:23:04,000 --> 01:23:08,000
Can't say. It's not an answerable question. It's a meaningless question.

41
01:23:08,000 --> 01:23:10,000
One person is a giant grin, everyone else is crying,

42
01:23:10,000 --> 01:23:14,000
you can't tell me who's the happiest person in this room?

43
01:23:14,000 --> 01:23:16,000
Seems like you could.

44
01:23:16,000 --> 01:23:20,000
So, it may be hard, there may be cases where it's hard to figure out.

45
01:23:20,000 --> 01:23:24,000
Maybe someone is carrying a great deal of sadness that they hide.

46
01:23:24,000 --> 01:23:28,000
But, that's different from saying it's conceptually impossible or meaningless.

47
01:23:28,000 --> 01:23:32,000
And here's the thing, once you say that you can't compare the happiness of two people,

48
01:23:32,000 --> 01:23:37,000
doesn't that also mean that you can't compare the happiness of an individual over time?

49
01:23:37,000 --> 01:23:41,000
Right? So, how can you say that I'm less happy now when I'm being tortured

50
01:23:41,000 --> 01:23:45,000
than before, than yesterday when I was free?

51
01:23:45,000 --> 01:23:49,000
If you can't say that the person who's being tortured is less happy than the person who's free,

52
01:23:49,000 --> 01:23:55,000
how can you say that you can compare the happiness of one individual in two different situations?

53
01:23:55,000 --> 01:23:59,000
So, again, that seems really weird to think that you can't do that.

54
01:23:59,000 --> 01:24:05,000
Would that be to say that an absolute utility comparison isn't possible,

55
01:24:05,000 --> 01:24:12,000
but a relative, or kind of a, just not an exact?

56
01:24:12,000 --> 01:24:16,000
You may say, well, it's hard being exact, sure, but,

57
01:24:16,000 --> 01:24:20,000
it's basically saying there's no fundamental conceptual problem with this.

58
01:24:20,000 --> 01:24:24,000
Now, if you've ever argued with Walter Block, and if you ever have,

59
01:24:24,000 --> 01:24:30,000
the argument will never stop until you let it give him the last word.

60
01:24:30,000 --> 01:24:32,000
That's for you, Walter Block student over there.

61
01:24:32,000 --> 01:24:35,000
We were talking about this. We said that exactly.

62
01:24:35,000 --> 01:24:39,000
Well, to be fair, Walter does have a whole article where he argues that the best standard of who's right

63
01:24:39,000 --> 01:24:44,000
is whoever has the last word, and he really does, and then he goes and he actually applies it

64
01:24:44,000 --> 01:24:49,000
empirically to about 50 different disputes, and he categorizes who got the last word,

65
01:24:49,000 --> 01:24:51,000
and then goes and ranks it and says so.

66
01:24:51,000 --> 01:24:54,000
Turns out that Austrians are more right empirically,

67
01:24:54,000 --> 01:24:59,000
because we, and often he personally, made sure to have the last word.

68
01:24:59,000 --> 01:25:06,000
So, he has a view, but I think it's far from being an idiosyncratic view that just says,

69
01:25:06,000 --> 01:25:09,000
look, there's two different ways of talking.

70
01:25:09,000 --> 01:25:11,000
There's the scientific way, and there's the normal way,

71
01:25:11,000 --> 01:25:17,000
and scientifically, it is impossible to go and ever say that something raises overall happiness,

72
01:25:17,000 --> 01:25:21,000
or makes an individual happy, or one happy individual is happier than another person,

73
01:25:21,000 --> 01:25:24,000
but of course we can do it in a normal way.

74
01:25:24,000 --> 01:25:29,000
My question for him is always, alright, well, is the normal way,

75
01:25:29,000 --> 01:25:32,000
when you're doing it in the normal way, is it still true?

76
01:25:32,000 --> 01:25:35,000
In that case, why don't we just talk the normal way?

77
01:25:35,000 --> 01:25:38,000
And yet, if it's not still true, then you haven't really solved anything.

78
01:25:38,000 --> 01:25:40,000
You've just bitten the bullet and just say yes,

79
01:25:40,000 --> 01:25:45,000
and then you're just, it's just a metaphor to say that the person being tortured is less happy

80
01:25:45,000 --> 01:25:47,000
than the person who's been free.

81
01:25:47,000 --> 01:25:49,000
That seems weird to me.

82
01:25:49,000 --> 01:25:50,000
Yes?

83
01:25:50,000 --> 01:25:56,000
At the risk of assuming two people, it would be as if you had two separate planets

84
01:25:56,000 --> 01:25:59,000
that could contact each other, or two separate countries even.

85
01:25:59,000 --> 01:26:03,000
Let's say you took planets, two different life forms with different biological means,

86
01:26:03,000 --> 01:26:05,000
and each planet operated on a one world currency.

87
01:26:05,000 --> 01:26:07,000
You could never compare the currency values of each one,

88
01:26:07,000 --> 01:26:11,000
because there was no possible commensurable thing.

89
01:26:11,000 --> 01:26:13,000
You could track the value of the currency over time.

90
01:26:13,000 --> 01:26:17,000
You could track the value of the global dollar versus the Mars coin,

91
01:26:17,000 --> 01:26:21,000
but you could never say how many global dollars does the Mars coin worth,

92
01:26:21,000 --> 01:26:25,000
because nothing on Mars is something that we'd ever buy.

93
01:26:25,000 --> 01:26:28,000
Right, of course you could still make a guess about what the exchange rate would be like

94
01:26:28,000 --> 01:26:31,000
if there were an exchange rate.

95
01:26:31,000 --> 01:26:33,000
So, conceptually it's still doable, and in fact,

96
01:26:33,000 --> 01:26:38,000
say look, I predict that if exchange ever opens up, that this is what the rate's going to be.

97
01:26:38,000 --> 01:26:44,000
And it seems meaningful, it may be that there's no way that it's never going to get decisively resolved,

98
01:26:44,000 --> 01:26:50,000
just like if you were to say, like if Klaus von Schaufenberg successfully assassinated Hitler,

99
01:26:50,000 --> 01:26:55,000
then the borders of Germany at the end of World War II would have wound up being very different.

100
01:26:55,000 --> 01:26:59,000
There's no way of ever checking that, yet it seems true.

101
01:26:59,000 --> 01:27:03,000
Of course you've never, maybe you've never heard of Schaufenberg in which case, I don't know.

102
01:27:03,000 --> 01:27:09,000
But most people who study the facts would say, yeah, it seems pretty likely.

103
01:27:09,000 --> 01:27:11,000
All right.

104
01:27:11,000 --> 01:27:13,000
Now, let's see.

105
01:27:13,000 --> 01:27:19,000
Now, one common thing for economists to say is, well, utilitarianism and Calgary's are almost the same.

106
01:27:19,000 --> 01:27:25,000
All that we're doing here is that utilitarianism is adjusting for the marginal utility of wealth.

107
01:27:25,000 --> 01:27:27,000
So, common thing to say.

108
01:27:27,000 --> 01:27:32,000
So, there's something to this, but it's hardly necessarily true.

109
01:27:32,000 --> 01:27:35,000
People might be willing to pay for things other than happiness,

110
01:27:35,000 --> 01:27:39,000
or there might be some people who just get very little happiness out of a dollar.

111
01:27:39,000 --> 01:27:43,000
Like, you know anyone who just seems to not really enjoy much of anything?

112
01:27:43,000 --> 01:27:46,000
And there's other people who just savor every experience.

113
01:27:46,000 --> 01:27:52,000
So, yeah, I mean, so, like, again, if you were utilitarian, you wouldn't just stop at redistribution from rich to poor.

114
01:27:52,000 --> 01:28:00,000
You might also say, let's also redistribute from people who have very unresponsive emotions

115
01:28:00,000 --> 01:28:02,000
to people who have very responsive emotions.

116
01:28:02,000 --> 01:28:07,000
Let's go and take stuff from people who seem to be the same regardless of what happens to them

117
01:28:07,000 --> 01:28:14,000
and give it to people who have, like, who really appreciate things or really dislike poverty.

118
01:28:14,000 --> 01:28:19,000
So, like, you know, probably, like, you know, cheerful people would get bigger redistribution.

119
01:28:19,000 --> 01:28:23,000
And the chronically miserable people, well, like, they're always going to be miserable, so what's the difference?

120
01:28:23,000 --> 01:28:26,000
Let's grow them blind.

121
01:28:26,000 --> 01:28:27,000
Right?

122
01:28:27,000 --> 01:28:32,000
Seems reasonable, and you probably have some friends that just seem miserable all the time.

123
01:28:32,000 --> 01:28:36,000
And then at some point you may say, why do I try to cheer you up?

124
01:28:36,000 --> 01:28:37,000
Nothing works.

125
01:28:37,000 --> 01:28:42,000
I'm going to go and put my energy into someone that does get cheered up when I do things for them.

126
01:28:42,000 --> 01:28:43,000
All right.

127
01:28:43,000 --> 01:28:47,000
And, of course, utilitarianism is often used to justify redistribution.

128
01:28:47,000 --> 01:28:51,000
But, you know, even on its own terms, there's problems with this.

129
01:28:51,000 --> 01:28:57,000
And then there is a classic philosopher's counter example, which is fun.

130
01:28:57,000 --> 01:29:00,000
I'm never going to mention it again, but you might want to hear about it.

131
01:29:00,000 --> 01:29:02,000
It's the utility monster.

132
01:29:02,000 --> 01:29:05,000
This is a genetically engineered creature with increasing marginal utility wealth.

133
01:29:05,000 --> 01:29:09,000
So every time you double its wealth, it gets more than a doubling of happiness.

134
01:29:09,000 --> 01:29:13,000
And all the other creatures in the universe have diminishing marginal utility wealth.

135
01:29:13,000 --> 01:29:20,000
So it's easy to come up with a story where actually the most efficient and the most utilitarian thing to do

136
01:29:20,000 --> 01:29:25,000
is to expropriate everyone else on Earth or in the universe, give it all to the utility monster.

137
01:29:25,000 --> 01:29:27,000
The rest of us die in horrible agony.

138
01:29:27,000 --> 01:29:28,000
But don't worry.

139
01:29:28,000 --> 01:29:35,000
That utility monster loves all your possessions so much that it's better, in utility terms, that we're all dead.

140
01:29:35,000 --> 01:29:39,000
And then you have plenty of other oddities here.

141
01:29:39,000 --> 01:29:46,000
So there's a long-running debate between average and total utilitarianism.

142
01:29:46,000 --> 01:29:50,000
Many people don't like the idea of total utilitarianism.

143
01:29:50,000 --> 01:29:58,000
It means that if you create a new life where the person is just barely better off than indifferent between being alive,

144
01:29:58,000 --> 01:29:59,000
that's a good thing.

145
01:29:59,000 --> 01:30:02,000
So it's a good thing to create very marginal lives.

146
01:30:02,000 --> 01:30:04,000
So many people say, no, that's crazy.

147
01:30:04,000 --> 01:30:08,000
Then there's the average utilitarianism has even weirder implications.

148
01:30:08,000 --> 01:30:11,000
So let's maximize the average utility in the world.

149
01:30:11,000 --> 01:30:15,000
Well, does that mean that we should just kill off everyone except the happiest person on Earth?

150
01:30:15,000 --> 01:30:18,000
What if he's a misanthrope so he doesn't get lonely?

151
01:30:18,000 --> 01:30:20,000
And then, oh, this is great.

152
01:30:20,000 --> 01:30:22,000
I've got a whole planet to myself.

153
01:30:22,000 --> 01:30:23,000
I'm so happy.

154
01:30:23,000 --> 01:30:27,000
Average utility would never have been this high when the world had other people on it.

155
01:30:27,000 --> 01:30:29,000
But now they're gone.

156
01:30:29,000 --> 01:30:31,000
Isn't that a Twilight Zone episode?

157
01:30:31,000 --> 01:30:33,000
And then his glasses break.

158
01:30:33,000 --> 01:30:34,000
Oh, yeah.

159
01:30:34,000 --> 01:30:39,000
I mean, he wasn't so much a misanthrope as he just liked books a lot and everyone was mean to him.

160
01:30:39,000 --> 01:30:43,000
He didn't actually revel in the deaths of others.

161
01:30:43,000 --> 01:30:46,000
He was pretty excited to go to that library.

162
01:30:46,000 --> 01:30:47,000
Yeah, he was.

163
01:30:47,000 --> 01:30:51,000
But you wouldn't have minded if there were other bookworms he could hang out with.

164
01:30:51,000 --> 01:30:53,000
He wouldn't have cared about that.

165
01:30:53,000 --> 01:31:02,000
Anyway, so I mention this just because there is an unusually high share of people at GMU who do economics of philosophy.

166
01:31:02,000 --> 01:31:06,000
So if any of this stuff interests you, there is a lot you can do here.

167
01:31:06,000 --> 01:31:14,000
It's actually likely you'll have a better career as being an economist who does some empirical work and also does some economics of philosophy after tenure.

168
01:31:14,000 --> 01:31:18,000
Probably a way better career than if you just try to be a pure philosopher doing this stuff.

169
01:31:18,000 --> 01:31:26,000
So this is sort of my general career advice to almost everyone is if you do math, just get a PhD in economics, get tenure in economics,

170
01:31:26,000 --> 01:31:29,000
and then call whatever you do the economics of that.

171
01:31:29,000 --> 01:31:36,000
And even if you never get another raise, you'll still do better than most history professors or sociologists or other people ever will.

172
01:31:36,000 --> 01:31:38,000
So worth considering.

173
01:31:38,000 --> 01:31:41,000
All right, so questions about utilitarianism?

174
01:31:41,000 --> 01:31:42,000
Everyone happy?

175
01:31:42,000 --> 01:31:43,000
All right, really happy.

176
01:31:43,000 --> 01:31:48,000
Not just willing to pay, but a genuine emotion of well-being.

177
01:31:48,000 --> 01:31:49,000
Okay.

178
01:31:49,000 --> 01:31:51,000
All right, then moving on.

179
01:31:51,000 --> 01:31:52,000
All right.

180
01:31:52,000 --> 01:31:56,000
The comparative institution's first and second best.

181
01:31:56,000 --> 01:32:00,000
All right, now I'm guessing that Walter Williams mentioned Harold Dempsetz to you.

182
01:32:00,000 --> 01:32:01,000
More than once.

183
01:32:01,000 --> 01:32:04,000
All right, yes, the great Harold Dempsetz.

184
01:32:04,000 --> 01:32:07,000
All right, and Harold Dempsetz had a bunch of papers.

185
01:32:07,000 --> 01:32:18,000
One of them, well, I think there's only one where he mentioned Buddhist theology, which was the paper on the Nirvana Fallacy.

186
01:32:18,000 --> 01:32:24,000
Now, if someone actually understands Buddhism, I think we'll say that he has a total misunderstanding of it, but that's not important.

187
01:32:24,000 --> 01:32:27,000
What's important is what he was talking about.

188
01:32:27,000 --> 01:32:39,000
So what Dempsetz called the Nirvana Fallacy is doing what pretends to be Calgary-Hicks efficiency analysis while selectively relaxing whatever constraints you don't feel like worrying about.

189
01:32:39,000 --> 01:32:45,000
In order to go, and then we hang it, why do you go and selectively ignore a bunch of constraints?

190
01:32:45,000 --> 01:32:48,000
To get the answer you want, of course.

191
01:32:48,000 --> 01:32:54,000
Again, of course, it may be that you do it because other people get mad at you if you have the wrong answer, so you do it that way.

192
01:32:54,000 --> 01:32:56,000
Maybe you do it just because no one else ever did.

193
01:32:56,000 --> 01:32:58,000
Maybe you are actually just subconscious.

194
01:32:58,000 --> 01:32:59,000
Maybe it's very conscious.

195
01:32:59,000 --> 01:33:01,000
But anyway, this is what he talked about.

196
01:33:01,000 --> 01:33:13,000
So you're supposedly doing this, but then you do a very sloppy job where you forget important constraints, and then you come up with an answer that doesn't make a lot of sense.

197
01:33:13,000 --> 01:33:16,000
All right, now, why was he making this claim?

198
01:33:16,000 --> 01:33:26,000
Well, at the time that Dempsetz was writing, there was something we now call old-style welfare economics where people just did really crummy Calgary-Hicks analysis

199
01:33:26,000 --> 01:33:37,000
where they ignored a bunch of constraints, and it's fair to say that suspiciously ignoring the constraints generally tilted the scales heavily towards government regulation.

200
01:33:37,000 --> 01:33:46,000
Right now, I'll say that when I was an undergraduate at UC Berkeley, Dempsetz's critique still applied wholly to most of the undergraduate classes.

201
01:33:46,000 --> 01:33:47,000
Really.

202
01:33:47,000 --> 01:33:55,000
So I can't tell you how many times I was in an undergraduate class where someone would just go and draw that triangle, draw the monopoly, show everybody an off-striangle,

203
01:33:55,000 --> 01:33:57,000
and then say, see, government's got you.

204
01:33:57,000 --> 01:33:59,000
Government must do something to fix this.

205
01:33:59,000 --> 01:34:02,000
And this is what government will solve the problem.

206
01:34:02,000 --> 01:34:11,000
And again, there's the ambiguity of in the real world or in this model, but the tone is always, well, yeah, like the real world.

207
01:34:11,000 --> 01:34:17,000
Kind of dogmatic, who will argue that you don't see how these triangles show that government is good.

208
01:34:17,000 --> 01:34:20,000
They're just geometrical shapes.

209
01:34:20,000 --> 01:34:25,000
They don't really show anything in the real world, at least not until you do some more work.

210
01:34:25,000 --> 01:34:33,000
So he was complaining about the shortcomings of government not being factored in and actually often just overhead not being figured in.

211
01:34:33,000 --> 01:34:39,000
So when someone says we need regulation, at minimum you say, well, how much are the regulators going to cost?

212
01:34:39,000 --> 01:34:40,000
Don't they need a building?

213
01:34:40,000 --> 01:34:42,000
And what about their phone bills?

214
01:34:42,000 --> 01:34:44,000
And just add up all that stuff.

215
01:34:44,000 --> 01:34:45,000
At minimum, you've got to put that in.

216
01:34:45,000 --> 01:34:53,000
Maybe all that stuff is bigger than the triangle, in which case, turns out it's not much of an argument for government regulation.

217
01:34:53,000 --> 01:34:55,000
All right.

218
01:34:55,000 --> 01:35:04,000
So, I'll say since 20 years after he wrote the article, I still saw this going on routinely at UC Berkeley, or at least classes for undergraduates.

219
01:35:04,000 --> 01:35:07,000
I don't think this is a straw man at the time.

220
01:35:07,000 --> 01:35:11,000
I think he actually is attacking a very common point of view.

221
01:35:11,000 --> 01:35:15,000
So I have a blog post called The Straw Man, Straw Man.

222
01:35:15,000 --> 01:35:22,000
And this is where people accuse you of attacking a straw man when in fact you're attacking an extremely popular argument.

223
01:35:22,000 --> 01:35:24,000
And they're like, look, it's not a straw man.

224
01:35:24,000 --> 01:35:25,000
Why doesn't he hold this?

225
01:35:25,000 --> 01:35:27,000
So I'm criticizing a view that people actually hold.

226
01:35:27,000 --> 01:35:29,000
How is that a straw man?

227
01:35:29,000 --> 01:35:34,000
So a straw man is supposed to be an argument so stupid that virtually nobody holds it.

228
01:35:34,000 --> 01:35:37,000
But there's a lot of popular stupid arguments.

229
01:35:37,000 --> 01:35:39,000
So what's wrong with those?

230
01:35:39,000 --> 01:35:40,000
Well, they're terrible.

231
01:35:40,000 --> 01:35:41,000
But what's wrong with attacking them?

232
01:35:41,000 --> 01:35:43,000
Nothing.

233
01:35:43,000 --> 01:35:44,000
They should be attacked.

234
01:35:44,000 --> 01:35:45,000
All right.

235
01:35:45,000 --> 01:35:48,000
Now this is a classic example of what Zebby says he's talking about.

236
01:35:48,000 --> 01:35:49,000
All right.

237
01:35:49,000 --> 01:35:50,000
Price greater than marginal cost.

238
01:35:50,000 --> 01:35:52,000
That's right here, right?

239
01:35:52,000 --> 01:35:56,000
So remember, as I said, the monopolist produces a marginal cost equals marginal revenue.

240
01:35:56,000 --> 01:36:00,000
And therefore, price is up here, marginal cost is down there, debt rate costs.

241
01:36:00,000 --> 01:36:01,000
Terrible.

242
01:36:01,000 --> 01:36:09,000
And I think that during my four years as an undergraduate, I saw maybe 500 debt rate loss triangles drawn.

243
01:36:09,000 --> 01:36:16,000
And always these triangles were to show market's bad and government can, should, will, and must solve this problem.

244
01:36:16,000 --> 01:36:19,000
And only a dogmatic fool would doubt it.

245
01:36:19,000 --> 01:36:20,000
All right.

246
01:36:20,000 --> 01:36:23,000
So it all comes down to this problem of price being greater than marginal cost.

247
01:36:23,000 --> 01:36:26,000
Now what is the classic remedy for this?

248
01:36:26,000 --> 01:36:28,000
It's a really simple one.

249
01:36:28,000 --> 01:36:30,000
So you've got monopoly.

250
01:36:30,000 --> 01:36:34,000
So I guess to me the simplest one is just trust busting down a perfect competition.

251
01:36:34,000 --> 01:36:36,000
But there's something much simpler here.

252
01:36:36,000 --> 01:36:38,000
Say, well, how about we just impose a price control?

253
01:36:38,000 --> 01:36:40,000
Price equals marginal cost price control.

254
01:36:40,000 --> 01:36:47,000
And in a monology, if you remember the way the monopoly model works, if you get that price control exactly right, it doesn't discourage production at all.

255
01:36:47,000 --> 01:36:51,000
Because essentially the monopolist treats that like his new demand curve.

256
01:36:51,000 --> 01:36:56,000
And then he just sets, you know, he produces until marginal cost equals that price.

257
01:36:56,000 --> 01:36:59,000
Because the price control, the whole idea is that it's flat.

258
01:36:59,000 --> 01:37:00,000
It's horizontal, right?

259
01:37:00,000 --> 01:37:02,000
Just like a perfect competition.

260
01:37:02,000 --> 01:37:03,000
All right.

261
01:37:03,000 --> 01:37:05,000
So you go and you set this price control.

262
01:37:05,000 --> 01:37:10,000
And then, and then you notice, and then the monopolist produces there, debt rate cost solved, and the problem.

263
01:37:10,000 --> 01:37:11,000
All right.

264
01:37:11,000 --> 01:37:14,000
So this is a classic Nirvana fallacy remedy.

265
01:37:14,000 --> 01:37:15,000
All right.

266
01:37:15,000 --> 01:37:19,000
Just think about all the problems of doing this in the real world.

267
01:37:19,000 --> 01:37:20,000
All right.

268
01:37:20,000 --> 01:37:30,000
Well, so, you know, they like to say, so, you know, the, this creates a secondary, the price control creates a secondary problem, which is, wait a second, why do we have a monopoly anyway?

269
01:37:30,000 --> 01:37:33,000
Is this really a constant returns to scale industry that has a monopoly?

270
01:37:33,000 --> 01:37:34,000
It's weird.

271
01:37:34,000 --> 01:37:37,000
How often do you get monopolies in constant returns to scale industries?

272
01:37:37,000 --> 01:37:39,000
Almost never.

273
01:37:39,000 --> 01:37:40,000
All right.

274
01:37:40,000 --> 01:37:42,000
So, and you say, yeah, yeah, well, yeah, okay, fine.

275
01:37:42,000 --> 01:37:43,000
All right.

276
01:37:43,000 --> 01:37:44,000
Well, yeah, there's some fixed costs.

277
01:37:44,000 --> 01:37:45,000
So what?

278
01:37:45,000 --> 01:37:54,000
Well, if you impose a constant, you know, you impose this, this, you know, you set pricing with a marginal cost, and there's fixed costs, then the firms are going to be losing money, right?

279
01:37:54,000 --> 01:37:55,000
Normally.

280
01:37:55,000 --> 01:37:56,000
Like, you can fiddle with it.

281
01:37:56,000 --> 01:38:02,000
But normally, if there's a fixed cost and there's a constant marginal cost, you make the price a marginal cost, so actually that case always.

282
01:38:02,000 --> 01:38:06,000
So if there's a fixed cost, constant marginal cost, you make the price a marginal cost, they will lose money.

283
01:38:06,000 --> 01:38:07,000
Predictably.

284
01:38:07,000 --> 01:38:09,000
They will lose the fixed cost.

285
01:38:09,000 --> 01:38:10,000
All right.

286
01:38:10,000 --> 01:38:19,000
So then you can say, well, you can't expect the industry to stay in business losing money forever, so then your great remedy isn't going to make the product available at marginal cost.

287
01:38:19,000 --> 01:38:22,000
Instead, it's going to remove the product from the market.

288
01:38:22,000 --> 01:38:24,000
Thanks a lot.

289
01:38:24,000 --> 01:38:25,000
Right?

290
01:38:25,000 --> 01:38:26,000
And, oh, well, come on.

291
01:38:26,000 --> 01:38:27,000
All right.

292
01:38:27,000 --> 01:38:28,000
Fine.

293
01:38:28,000 --> 01:38:30,000
Well, we'll go and give them a subsidy in order to make up that fixed cost.

294
01:38:30,000 --> 01:38:33,000
Let's go and hand them a bag of money from the fixed cost, just enough to keep them in business.

295
01:38:33,000 --> 01:38:34,000
All right.

296
01:38:34,000 --> 01:38:35,000
Now everything's great.

297
01:38:35,000 --> 01:38:36,000
All right.

298
01:38:36,000 --> 01:38:39,000
And then, all right, well, how are you going to fund these subsidies?

299
01:38:39,000 --> 01:38:42,000
Where's the money for the subsidies coming from?

300
01:38:42,000 --> 01:38:43,000
Oh, my God.

301
01:38:43,000 --> 01:38:44,000
You're full of questions.

302
01:38:44,000 --> 01:38:46,000
Why do I have to deal with all this?

303
01:38:46,000 --> 01:38:51,000
Look, I have my brilliant remedy, and now you're going and asking me a bunch of questions like where's the money supposed to come from?

304
01:38:51,000 --> 01:38:52,000
All right.

305
01:38:52,000 --> 01:38:54,000
Well, of course, like, well, yes, you've caught us.

306
01:38:54,000 --> 01:38:56,000
The money's going to come from taxes.

307
01:38:56,000 --> 01:38:58,000
But wait a second.

308
01:38:58,000 --> 01:39:03,000
Isn't the whole thing that taxes do create a gap between price and marginal cost?

309
01:39:03,000 --> 01:39:12,000
So your solution to this problem of price being greater than marginal cost is ultimately to go and impose a tax, which then creates a gap between price and marginal cost.

310
01:39:12,000 --> 01:39:18,000
Right now, in particularly obtuse theorists, they say, no, no, no, we're assuming they're all on some taxes.

311
01:39:18,000 --> 01:39:19,000
All right.

312
01:39:19,000 --> 01:39:21,000
And where are the role of those?

313
01:39:21,000 --> 01:39:22,000
Nowhere.

314
01:39:22,000 --> 01:39:29,000
So maybe you shouldn't have talked down to people so quickly before when you proposed your supposedly brilliant remedy to the problem.

315
01:39:29,000 --> 01:39:33,000
And this is what Dem says was getting at.

316
01:39:33,000 --> 01:39:34,000
All right.

317
01:39:34,000 --> 01:39:35,000
Okay.

318
01:39:35,000 --> 01:39:36,000
So questions?

319
01:39:36,000 --> 01:39:37,000
Right.

320
01:39:37,000 --> 01:39:43,000
Again, like, this error seems so basic to me that I know there's plenty of economists who'll say no one was that dumb.

321
01:39:43,000 --> 01:39:50,000
Not only are there plenty of people that dumb, there are plenty of textbooks that are written this badly, and people teach undergraduate stuff.

322
01:39:50,000 --> 01:39:58,000
And maybe there's no actual professor that really believes something that dumb, but if you're teaching it to undergraduates, then there's still a problem.

323
01:39:58,000 --> 01:40:01,000
But I think if you're teaching it to undergraduates as if it is dumb, then there's a problem.


1
01:40:00,000 --> 01:40:02,000
is descriptive of the world.

2
01:40:02,000 --> 01:40:04,000
And I know I was taught this many times.

3
01:40:04,000 --> 01:40:08,120
So for someone to say no one believes this,

4
01:40:08,120 --> 01:40:09,480
either they believe it or they were

5
01:40:09,480 --> 01:40:12,160
lying to a bunch of repressionable college students.

6
01:40:12,160 --> 01:40:15,360
Both of them pretty bad.

7
01:40:15,360 --> 01:40:15,860
This is me.

8
01:40:15,860 --> 01:40:16,360
Not lying.

9
01:40:16,360 --> 01:40:17,720
This is me.

10
01:40:17,720 --> 01:40:18,640
I'm not lying.

11
01:40:18,640 --> 01:40:20,520
I was just using words in two different ways.

12
01:40:20,520 --> 01:40:22,200
Nobody would understand the difference.

13
01:40:25,280 --> 01:40:26,960
Now what is Dempset's alternative?

14
01:40:26,960 --> 01:40:29,120
He has an alternative that he calls the comparative

15
01:40:29,120 --> 01:40:30,040
institutions approach.

16
01:40:36,080 --> 01:40:38,440
And this begins by saying nothing in the real world

17
01:40:38,440 --> 01:40:40,720
is perfectly counter-exhibition.

18
01:40:40,720 --> 01:40:41,760
It's not doable.

19
01:40:41,760 --> 01:40:44,000
There's always going to be some debit cost

20
01:40:44,000 --> 01:40:45,720
no matter what you do in the real world.

21
01:40:45,720 --> 01:40:47,600
So you just have to live with it.

22
01:40:47,600 --> 01:40:51,080
And so instead you should be asking what fails least badly.

23
01:40:51,080 --> 01:40:53,880
What fails least badly?

24
01:40:53,880 --> 01:41:00,240
And there is a parable associated with this.

25
01:41:00,240 --> 01:41:00,880
No, allegory.

26
01:41:00,880 --> 01:41:02,040
So you can add allegory.

27
01:41:02,040 --> 01:41:05,280
So allegory is something like George Orwell's Animal Farm

28
01:41:05,280 --> 01:41:08,200
where the characters stand for other things.

29
01:41:08,200 --> 01:41:09,680
So if you've ever read Animal Farm,

30
01:41:09,680 --> 01:41:17,200
you know that Snowball is Trotsky and Napoleon is Stalin.

31
01:41:17,200 --> 01:41:20,400
And then the Raven is supposed to be Rasputin, I've heard.

32
01:41:20,400 --> 01:41:23,240
And the second time they blow up,

33
01:41:23,240 --> 01:41:25,800
the windmill, that's World War II.

34
01:41:25,800 --> 01:41:26,600
All right, anyway.

35
01:41:26,600 --> 01:41:29,640
So an allegory is a story where there's actually

36
01:41:29,640 --> 01:41:34,320
a mapping from the fable you're telling into the real world.

37
01:41:34,320 --> 01:41:35,760
Very close one.

38
01:41:35,760 --> 01:41:38,560
So anyway, there is a fable associated

39
01:41:38,560 --> 01:41:40,520
with this comparative institutions idea called

40
01:41:40,520 --> 01:41:41,880
The Tale of the Emperor.

41
01:41:41,880 --> 01:41:42,760
Or the allegory.

42
01:41:42,760 --> 01:41:44,800
The allegory goes like this.

43
01:41:44,800 --> 01:41:47,680
The Emperor Rome announces a great musical contest

44
01:41:47,680 --> 01:41:50,560
where he summons all the best musicians in the empire

45
01:41:50,560 --> 01:41:52,600
to come to his palace and play for him.

46
01:41:52,600 --> 01:41:55,240
But only two performers show up.

47
01:41:55,240 --> 01:41:57,680
The first performer plays this whole song

48
01:41:57,680 --> 01:41:59,320
and he misses one note.

49
01:41:59,320 --> 01:42:02,600
And as soon as he misses a note, the Roman Emperor says,

50
01:42:02,600 --> 01:42:03,640
the contest is over.

51
01:42:03,640 --> 01:42:05,120
The second guy wins.

52
01:42:05,120 --> 01:42:08,160
He's the best singer, the best musician in the realm.

53
01:42:08,160 --> 01:42:09,920
The end.

54
01:42:09,920 --> 01:42:12,600
That's the entire allegory.

55
01:42:12,600 --> 01:42:14,280
Now, what is the mapping?

56
01:42:14,280 --> 01:42:15,720
Well, the Roman Emperor is supposed

57
01:42:15,720 --> 01:42:17,680
to be the economics profession.

58
01:42:17,680 --> 01:42:20,120
And the first musician is supposed to be the market.

59
01:42:20,120 --> 01:42:23,200
And the second musician is supposed to be the government.

60
01:42:23,200 --> 01:42:28,360
And this is especially associated with public choice.

61
01:42:28,360 --> 01:42:30,560
The claim is that a lot of economists

62
01:42:30,560 --> 01:42:33,480
go and look at markets, find one small problem,

63
01:42:33,480 --> 01:42:35,800
and then say, look, government is the solution here,

64
01:42:35,800 --> 01:42:37,400
without ever actually, empirically

65
01:42:37,400 --> 01:42:39,000
studying government.

66
01:42:39,000 --> 01:42:41,120
That's what corresponds to giving them

67
01:42:41,120 --> 01:42:44,200
the prize for ever listening to their music.

68
01:42:44,200 --> 01:42:46,080
And of course, the whole idea of public choice

69
01:42:46,080 --> 01:42:48,560
is we are going to empirically study government.

70
01:42:48,560 --> 01:42:50,160
And then when we empirically study it,

71
01:42:50,160 --> 01:42:52,160
we may find that it's even worse.

72
01:42:52,160 --> 01:42:53,560
It might miss two notes.

73
01:42:53,560 --> 01:42:55,960
It might miss all the notes.

74
01:42:55,960 --> 01:43:00,200
So we'll come back to that in the back as the tale of the emperor.

75
01:43:00,200 --> 01:43:02,080
All right, so questions about that?

76
01:43:04,840 --> 01:43:09,760
So in that case, last concept, the concept of the second best.

77
01:43:09,760 --> 01:43:13,400
So when you add more constraints to a standard problem,

78
01:43:13,400 --> 01:43:15,960
it often turns out the original option was no longer feasible.

79
01:43:15,960 --> 01:43:19,440
So think about how you drove to school tonight.

80
01:43:19,440 --> 01:43:21,720
You were already optimizing a bunch of things, right?

81
01:43:21,720 --> 01:43:23,400
Now, I suppose I add on the constraints.

82
01:43:23,400 --> 01:43:26,520
You also have to pick up some Kentucky fried chicken for me.

83
01:43:26,520 --> 01:43:28,840
All right, now, does anyone actually drive exactly

84
01:43:28,840 --> 01:43:31,440
past a KFC on their way here?

85
01:43:31,440 --> 01:43:32,600
Pop-up.

86
01:43:32,600 --> 01:43:33,480
What's that?

87
01:43:33,480 --> 01:43:35,160
You're not passing the pop-up.

88
01:43:35,160 --> 01:43:36,080
No, no, no, no, no.

89
01:43:36,080 --> 01:43:36,580
Not good.

90
01:43:36,580 --> 01:43:38,720
It's got to be KFC.

91
01:43:38,720 --> 01:43:40,440
All right, so as a result of this,

92
01:43:40,440 --> 01:43:42,160
if I impose this as a constraint on you,

93
01:43:42,160 --> 01:43:45,920
what will this automatically do to your commuting time?

94
01:43:45,920 --> 01:43:47,340
It will raise your commuting time

95
01:43:47,340 --> 01:43:50,400
when I add an extra constraint, unless it just so likely

96
01:43:50,400 --> 01:43:54,280
happens that my constraint corresponds

97
01:43:54,280 --> 01:43:55,520
to what you were doing anyway.

98
01:43:55,520 --> 01:43:58,000
But of course, that would almost never happen.

99
01:43:58,000 --> 01:44:00,720
So that is a general idea in optimization.

100
01:44:00,720 --> 01:44:02,180
Whenever you add a new constraint,

101
01:44:02,180 --> 01:44:05,840
you should expect that your last optimal will no longer

102
01:44:05,840 --> 01:44:08,420
be feasible, and you're going to have to do something that is

103
01:44:08,420 --> 01:44:12,320
harder or takes more time or is more painful.

104
01:44:12,320 --> 01:44:15,960
And economists will often refer to the original optimum

105
01:44:15,960 --> 01:44:19,680
as a first-best solution.

106
01:44:19,680 --> 01:44:21,800
So the original optimum before you

107
01:44:21,800 --> 01:44:23,880
add the extra constraints first-best,

108
01:44:23,880 --> 01:44:28,720
and then the second one, the thing

109
01:44:28,720 --> 01:44:31,720
that is optimal to do after one more constraint has been piled

110
01:44:31,720 --> 01:44:35,120
on your to-do list, a second-best.

111
01:44:35,120 --> 01:44:36,960
So an example.

112
01:44:36,960 --> 01:44:39,600
Economists often think a price equals marginal cost

113
01:44:39,600 --> 01:44:41,840
is first-best, until you say, yeah, well,

114
01:44:41,840 --> 01:44:43,680
there's going to be a constraint that firms

115
01:44:43,680 --> 01:44:46,240
can't lose money forever.

116
01:44:46,240 --> 01:44:47,680
Can't lose money forever.

117
01:44:47,680 --> 01:44:49,720
In that case, a second-best optimum

118
01:44:49,720 --> 01:44:52,480
might be price equals to average cost,

119
01:44:52,480 --> 01:44:55,840
even in an industry where you've got declining costs.

120
01:44:55,840 --> 01:44:59,200
Because given the constraint that firms

121
01:44:59,200 --> 01:45:01,840
can't lose money forever, what are they supposed to do?

122
01:45:01,840 --> 01:45:02,520
Accelerate them.

123
01:45:02,520 --> 01:45:04,400
You can't go and make them price a marginal cost,

124
01:45:04,400 --> 01:45:06,140
because they'll be driven out of business,

125
01:45:06,140 --> 01:45:08,200
and far worse to have zero firms that

126
01:45:08,200 --> 01:45:12,160
have monopoly charging more than marginal cost, right?

127
01:45:12,160 --> 01:45:14,560
So there's that.

128
01:45:14,560 --> 01:45:15,080
All right.

129
01:45:15,080 --> 01:45:16,560
And then occasionally, you'll hear economists

130
01:45:16,560 --> 01:45:18,800
say really weird things like, this is not first-best

131
01:45:18,800 --> 01:45:19,440
or second-best.

132
01:45:19,440 --> 01:45:21,000
It's more like 15th-best.

133
01:45:21,000 --> 01:45:24,040
And this is when they are talking about a very thorny

134
01:45:24,040 --> 01:45:26,240
real-world problem, where there are

135
01:45:26,240 --> 01:45:29,000
so many different constraints that they have to deal with.

136
01:45:29,000 --> 01:45:30,960
And of course, normally, it isn't literally

137
01:45:30,960 --> 01:45:32,560
14 extra constraints.

138
01:45:32,560 --> 01:45:34,560
Normally, it's just, look, so hard.

139
01:45:34,560 --> 01:45:37,600
There's so many different people causing problems,

140
01:45:37,600 --> 01:45:39,960
so many different complex issues.

141
01:45:39,960 --> 01:45:42,960
Someone's talking about privatizing telecom.

142
01:45:42,960 --> 01:45:45,880
Look, we've got to deal with our universal access requirement.

143
01:45:45,880 --> 01:45:51,040
We've got to deal with the minority business requirement.

144
01:45:51,040 --> 01:45:54,400
We've got to deal with a bundling issue.

145
01:45:54,400 --> 01:45:56,680
So, oh my god, it's so terrible.

146
01:45:56,680 --> 01:45:59,400
So anyway, if you've ever heard economists talk in these terms,

147
01:45:59,400 --> 01:46:02,080
that's what they're talking about.

148
01:46:02,080 --> 01:46:02,580
Questions?

149
01:46:02,580 --> 01:46:04,960
Anyone happy with that?

150
01:46:04,960 --> 01:46:08,280
Everyone's so tired that you no longer feel happy or unhappy

151
01:46:08,280 --> 01:46:10,760
about economics.

152
01:46:10,760 --> 01:46:11,920
Of course not.

153
01:46:11,920 --> 01:46:13,600
You will never be in the position

154
01:46:13,600 --> 01:46:16,280
where you cannot feel happy or unhappy about economics.

155
01:46:16,280 --> 01:46:17,680
It's always a pure joy.

156
01:46:20,240 --> 01:46:22,680
And then moving on, so last topic

157
01:46:22,680 --> 01:46:24,040
in economics and philosophy we're

158
01:46:24,040 --> 01:46:25,840
going to spend much time on.

159
01:46:25,840 --> 01:46:27,960
So moral philosophy and efficiency.

160
01:46:27,960 --> 01:46:30,760
So who cares about efficiency anyway?

161
01:46:30,760 --> 01:46:33,040
What difference does it possibly make?

162
01:46:33,040 --> 01:46:35,160
So is there anyone on earth who seriously believes

163
01:46:35,160 --> 01:46:38,960
that the right action is always the counter-insufficient one?

164
01:46:38,960 --> 01:46:41,040
The answer is yes, there are at least a few people

165
01:46:41,040 --> 01:46:43,040
who have endorsed this view.

166
01:46:43,040 --> 01:46:46,680
This Harvard law and economics guy, Steve Chabell,

167
01:46:46,680 --> 01:46:48,920
I was not there, but I heard from a liable source

168
01:46:48,920 --> 01:46:51,760
that he referred to people who care about anything

169
01:46:51,760 --> 01:46:55,040
other than counter-insufficiency as the dark side.

170
01:46:55,040 --> 01:46:57,440
The whole talk was, those on the dark side

171
01:46:57,440 --> 01:47:00,080
believe that things other than efficiency matter.

172
01:47:00,080 --> 01:47:01,720
But think about what that would mean.

173
01:47:01,720 --> 01:47:03,960
It means sometimes we'd have to do inefficient things.

174
01:47:03,960 --> 01:47:05,880
And that's really inefficient.

175
01:47:05,880 --> 01:47:07,400
So no.

176
01:47:07,400 --> 01:47:10,520
All right, so maybe you've spent better than that.

177
01:47:10,520 --> 01:47:12,800
And if you go and read early Richard Posner, not

178
01:47:12,800 --> 01:47:14,920
modern Posner, who has a very confusing view,

179
01:47:14,920 --> 01:47:17,520
but early Posner, does sound like he just

180
01:47:17,520 --> 01:47:19,720
thinks that whatever is counter-insufficient is exactly

181
01:47:19,720 --> 01:47:21,600
what you ought to do.

182
01:47:21,600 --> 01:47:23,360
So there are some people like this.

183
01:47:23,360 --> 01:47:26,240
And Robin Hanson occasionally talks this way, too,

184
01:47:26,240 --> 01:47:28,680
although his real view is a bit more subtle than that.

185
01:47:28,680 --> 01:47:33,480
In particular, he doesn't have to do what's counter-insufficient.

186
01:47:33,480 --> 01:47:36,280
But in some way, social rules should still

187
01:47:36,280 --> 01:47:37,280
be counter-insufficient.

188
01:47:37,280 --> 01:47:39,360
And my question is, well, if no individual

189
01:47:39,360 --> 01:47:41,600
is obligated to do the counter-insufficient thing,

190
01:47:41,600 --> 01:47:45,160
then how can society be obligated to do it?

191
01:47:45,160 --> 01:47:47,080
And I think his answer is, no, you're

192
01:47:47,080 --> 01:47:52,280
moralizing a claim that I don't need as a moral claim.

193
01:47:52,280 --> 01:47:53,920
But then what is it exactly?

194
01:47:53,920 --> 01:47:55,880
So again, Robin actually calls his view

195
01:47:55,880 --> 01:47:58,840
dealism and is sort of the view that we should do it.

196
01:47:58,840 --> 01:48:00,600
Like, counter-insufficiency is a good way

