All right.

459
01:58:26,840 --> 01:58:28,840
So you've got some consumer surplus here.

460
01:58:28,840 --> 01:58:32,840
So maybe you're, you know, like plus $5 billion in consumer

461
01:58:32,840 --> 01:58:33,840
surplus.

462
01:58:33,840 --> 01:58:34,840
All right.

463
01:58:34,840 --> 01:58:36,840
And then you take a look at this and you say, well, I mean,

464
01:58:36,840 --> 01:58:41,840
it seems like this is not really going to be worth it because,

465
01:58:41,840 --> 01:58:43,840
let's see, because basically we went and paid a billion in

466
01:58:43,840 --> 01:58:48,840
subsidies in order to go and transfer $600 billion to producers

467
01:58:48,840 --> 01:58:50,840
and to consumers.

468
01:58:50,840 --> 01:58:51,840
Right.

469
01:58:51,840 --> 01:58:52,840
Something like that.

470
01:58:52,840 --> 01:58:53,840
Right.

471
01:58:53,840 --> 01:58:57,840
And, you know, so anyway, so, you know, this is a way that a

472
01:58:57,840 --> 01:59:00,840
lot of industrial policy analysis works out where they say,

473
01:59:00,840 --> 01:59:02,840
yeah, we paid a subsidy, and then we looked at the profit

474
01:59:02,840 --> 01:59:04,840
with the consumer surplus, and the subsidy is greater than

475
01:59:04,840 --> 01:59:06,840
those, so it was a bad subsidy.

476
01:59:06,840 --> 01:59:11,840
And then there is actually a way that people will go and defend

477
01:59:11,840 --> 01:59:14,840
the industrial policy and say, no, it does pass the cost

478
01:59:14,840 --> 01:59:15,840
benefit test.

479
01:59:15,840 --> 01:59:17,840
And it's a pretty weird one.

480
01:59:17,840 --> 01:59:21,840
But when it's one that you would never want to make in public,

481
01:59:21,840 --> 01:59:23,840
but it's totally sound.

482
01:59:23,840 --> 01:59:24,840
And what's the argument?

483
01:59:24,840 --> 01:59:29,840
It's to say, yeah, well, they lost money, but here's what you

484
01:59:29,840 --> 01:59:30,840
have to remember.

485
01:59:30,840 --> 01:59:32,840
Actually, this would matter.

486
01:59:32,840 --> 01:59:34,840
I can even make that a plus $5 billion.

487
01:59:34,840 --> 01:59:38,840
So, yeah, the subsidy exceeded the profit and the consumer

488
01:59:38,840 --> 01:59:40,840
surplus, but here's the thing.

489
01:59:40,840 --> 01:59:42,840
The workers are grossly overpaid.

490
01:59:42,840 --> 01:59:45,840
The workers are grossly overpaid.

491
01:59:45,840 --> 01:59:49,840
The workers' opportunity cost is, say, a billion dollars less.

492
01:59:49,840 --> 01:59:51,840
They're getting paid like a billion dollars less than their

493
01:59:51,840 --> 01:59:53,840
actual next best alternative.

494
01:59:53,840 --> 01:59:57,840
So there's actually a hidden plus $1 billion in producer

495
01:59:57,840 --> 01:60:17,840
surplus that we work with.


1
01:60:00,000 --> 01:60:01,840
accounting because we were forgetting

2
01:60:01,840 --> 01:60:04,280
that the workers were overpaid.

3
01:60:04,280 --> 01:60:06,200
And then we're not paying a billion

4
01:60:06,200 --> 01:60:08,360
to go transfer $0.6 billion.

5
01:60:08,360 --> 01:60:11,680
We're paying a billion to transfer $1.6 billion.

6
01:60:11,680 --> 01:60:14,760
And all we need to do is admit that the workers are actually

7
01:60:14,760 --> 01:60:16,720
totally, grossly, grossly overpaid

8
01:60:16,720 --> 01:60:18,480
relative to the qualifications.

9
01:60:18,480 --> 01:60:23,200
And then we got a total cost-benefit analysis.

10
01:60:23,200 --> 01:60:27,000
This was, I think, Bill Clinton's first CEA head

11
01:60:27,000 --> 01:60:28,640
was Lyndon Lawrence Tyson.

12
01:60:28,640 --> 01:60:31,320
And she actually did cost-benefit analysis

13
01:60:31,320 --> 01:60:33,040
like this, arguing for investor policy.

14
01:60:33,040 --> 01:60:35,380
But again, it's one that never got much public attention.

15
01:60:35,380 --> 01:60:39,080
Because normally, when you want to defend anything

16
01:60:39,080 --> 01:60:41,600
that you're subsidizing, do you say that the workers

17
01:60:41,600 --> 01:60:43,480
are grossly overpaid?

18
01:60:43,480 --> 01:60:45,040
No.

19
01:60:45,040 --> 01:60:47,120
What you say is, they earn every penny.

20
01:60:47,120 --> 01:60:48,620
But if they're earning every penny,

21
01:60:48,620 --> 01:60:51,640
then counter-excess is a terrible idea.

22
01:60:51,640 --> 01:60:54,440
In order to make it a good idea, you just say, no.

23
01:60:54,440 --> 01:60:55,680
I mean, come on.

24
01:60:55,680 --> 01:60:57,180
They're totally unskilled workers

25
01:60:57,180 --> 01:60:59,080
and they're making $50 an hour.

26
01:60:59,080 --> 01:61:02,640
So you've got to remember that before you pull the plug on it.

27
01:61:02,640 --> 01:61:06,160
It's very important.

28
01:61:06,160 --> 01:61:08,080
Another analogous case of this is,

29
01:61:08,080 --> 01:61:10,160
when people are doing cost-benefit analysis,

30
01:61:10,160 --> 01:61:12,560
they will occasionally do something really crummy,

31
01:61:12,560 --> 01:61:13,800
which is say, well, look.

32
01:61:13,800 --> 01:61:15,360
This is going to create 100 jobs.

33
01:61:15,360 --> 01:61:18,840
And those workers are going to get, say, $10 million.

34
01:61:18,840 --> 01:61:22,720
And so that $10 million is a transfer to those workers.

35
01:61:22,720 --> 01:61:25,040
Is the $10 million that you give to those workers,

36
01:61:25,040 --> 01:61:29,000
like their salary is $10 million, is that really,

37
01:61:29,000 --> 01:61:31,200
economically speaking, a transfer of $10 million?

38
01:61:31,200 --> 01:61:31,700
No.

39
01:61:31,700 --> 01:61:32,360
No, why not?

40
01:61:32,360 --> 01:61:33,520
Because the workers have an opportunity cost.

41
01:61:33,520 --> 01:61:35,200
Yeah, they have an opportunity cost.

42
01:61:35,200 --> 01:61:36,900
So the workers, the next best alternative

43
01:61:36,900 --> 01:61:39,760
is actually to make $10 million.

44
01:61:39,760 --> 01:61:43,360
Then you actually haven't transferred anything to them.

45
01:61:43,360 --> 01:61:45,320
And if you're overpaying them by 5%,

46
01:61:45,320 --> 01:61:47,960
then there's a 5% transfer to them.

47
01:61:47,960 --> 01:61:49,560
But it's much less.

48
01:61:49,560 --> 01:61:54,080
So any time someone talks about, we

49
01:61:54,080 --> 01:61:57,480
have to think about all the benefits of all the workers,

50
01:61:57,480 --> 01:61:59,400
the benefits is not the salary.

51
01:61:59,400 --> 01:62:01,560
The benefit is the difference between the salary

52
01:62:01,560 --> 01:62:05,440
and the opportunity cost, which, again,

53
01:62:05,440 --> 01:62:06,680
goes back to the same point.

54
01:62:06,680 --> 01:62:08,180
If their opportunity cost is nothing

55
01:62:08,180 --> 01:62:10,760
because they're incompetent, then, yeah,

56
01:62:10,760 --> 01:62:13,920
then it's right to count it that way.

57
01:62:13,920 --> 01:62:15,480
So we know very often when economists

58
01:62:15,480 --> 01:62:18,240
talk about the distributional effects of deregulation,

59
01:62:18,240 --> 01:62:20,160
a lot of them say, well, a whole lot of lawyers

60
01:62:20,160 --> 01:62:20,800
are going to lose their jobs.

61
01:62:20,800 --> 01:62:23,000
And then they sometimes even put in the whole salary

62
01:62:23,000 --> 01:62:23,880
of the lawyer.

63
01:62:23,880 --> 01:62:25,580
It's like, well, I think these salaries,

64
01:62:25,580 --> 01:62:27,520
there's nothing else on Earth they can do.

65
01:62:27,520 --> 01:62:29,920
For obviously, most of them are going to just go and become

66
01:62:29,920 --> 01:62:31,920
a different kind of lawyer.

67
01:62:31,920 --> 01:62:34,200
But suppose that there were a massive reduction

68
01:62:34,200 --> 01:62:35,240
in the number of lawyers.

69
01:62:35,240 --> 01:62:38,360
Are those guys just going to go and become beggars?

70
01:62:38,360 --> 01:62:39,320
They'll find something.

71
01:62:39,320 --> 01:62:42,960
Almost all of them will find something else to do.

72
01:62:42,960 --> 01:62:46,360
So a key implication of cost-benefit analysis,

73
01:62:46,360 --> 01:62:49,440
which is very rarely understood, even by people doing it,

74
01:62:49,440 --> 01:62:51,560
and again, why is this counterintuitive to think

75
01:62:51,560 --> 01:62:54,200
of the way that you want to defend something in cost-benefit

76
01:62:54,200 --> 01:62:56,840
terms and say the workers are overpaid?

77
01:62:56,840 --> 01:62:59,000
Doesn't sound like a normal argument.

78
01:62:59,000 --> 01:63:01,640
It's just a sound argument, or at least it's a valid argument.

79
01:63:01,640 --> 01:63:05,040
It's one that is consistent with the system.

80
01:63:05,040 --> 01:63:07,760
Whereas if you say the workers deserve every penny they get,

81
01:63:07,760 --> 01:63:09,600
that's when you say, oh, well, in that case,

82
01:63:09,600 --> 01:63:11,760
let's not pay them.

83
01:63:11,760 --> 01:63:14,080
It only makes sense to pay them if they don't deserve

84
01:63:14,080 --> 01:63:15,600
most of what they're getting.

85
01:63:15,600 --> 01:63:19,360
Then it passes the cost-benefit test, otherwise not.

86
01:63:19,360 --> 01:63:22,440
Other questions?

87
01:63:22,440 --> 01:63:23,840
OK, are anyone happy?

88
01:63:23,840 --> 01:63:25,040
Anyone unhappy?

89
01:63:25,040 --> 01:63:27,680
All right, now let's move into probability,

90
01:63:27,680 --> 01:63:29,600
objective and subjective.

91
01:63:29,600 --> 01:63:34,680
So probability language lets us quantify uncertainty.

92
01:63:34,680 --> 01:63:36,720
So there's more to say in an uncertain world

93
01:63:36,720 --> 01:63:39,640
than I don't know.

94
01:63:39,640 --> 01:63:40,720
You could answer that way.

95
01:63:40,720 --> 01:63:42,480
So what are the odds that you get struck by lightning?

96
01:63:42,480 --> 01:63:43,320
I don't know.

97
01:63:43,320 --> 01:63:44,880
What are the odds that Trump will still be president tomorrow?

98
01:63:44,880 --> 01:63:45,880
I don't know.

99
01:63:45,880 --> 01:63:48,040
What are the odds that you eat dinner tonight?

100
01:63:48,040 --> 01:63:49,080
I don't know that either.

101
01:63:49,080 --> 01:63:50,640
I don't know anything.

102
01:63:50,640 --> 01:63:53,480
So since it's not certain, it's not impossible,

103
01:63:53,480 --> 01:63:55,760
I just plead the fifth.

104
01:63:55,760 --> 01:63:57,240
You can do that.

105
01:63:57,240 --> 01:64:00,520
But there's a lot more that you can say in an uncertain world.

106
01:64:00,520 --> 01:64:04,600
Now there's a couple different versions of probability theory.

107
01:64:04,600 --> 01:64:07,800
So the first one, which is controversial with almost no

108
01:64:07,800 --> 01:64:12,480
one, is called objective probability theory.

109
01:64:12,480 --> 01:64:13,880
Objective probability theory.

110
01:64:17,240 --> 01:64:19,000
Objective probability theory.

111
01:64:19,000 --> 01:64:19,640
All right.

112
01:64:19,640 --> 01:64:23,840
So this just says that even when you don't know what will happen,

113
01:64:23,840 --> 01:64:26,560
you're still allowed to talk about the relative frequencies

114
01:64:26,560 --> 01:64:29,600
of observed events in the past.

115
01:64:29,600 --> 01:64:31,920
So I don't know what's going to happen,

116
01:64:31,920 --> 01:64:35,320
but what I can say is that Democrats

117
01:64:35,320 --> 01:64:38,960
have won the popular vote in four of the last six elections.

118
01:64:38,960 --> 01:64:41,920
I think it might be now five of the last six.

119
01:64:41,920 --> 01:64:42,480
All right.

120
01:64:42,480 --> 01:64:44,600
So you can say, so the probability,

121
01:64:44,600 --> 01:64:46,440
the objective probability that Democrats

122
01:64:46,440 --> 01:64:51,680
are going to win the popular vote will be five of six, say.

123
01:64:51,680 --> 01:64:54,280
Or of course, you do this for actuarial tables.

124
01:64:54,280 --> 01:64:57,920
Are you going to live to be 70?

125
01:64:57,920 --> 01:64:58,800
I don't know.

126
01:64:58,800 --> 01:65:00,640
But what I can say is that the fraction

127
01:65:00,640 --> 01:65:04,560
of people in your demographic who make it to 70 in the past

128
01:65:04,560 --> 01:65:05,800
has been this.

129
01:65:05,800 --> 01:65:08,320
And that would be the objective probability.

130
01:65:08,320 --> 01:65:13,080
Or you could do this with dice or with cards.

131
01:65:13,080 --> 01:65:14,920
In all these cases, you can say, look,

132
01:65:14,920 --> 01:65:17,240
there's an objective probability.

133
01:65:17,240 --> 01:65:21,640
So in principle, you'd have to actually roll the dice

134
01:65:21,640 --> 01:65:22,160
empirically.

135
01:65:22,160 --> 01:65:25,840
Then say empirically, the six-sided die

136
01:65:25,840 --> 01:65:28,280
comes out one 1-6th of the time, two 1-6th of the time,

137
01:65:28,280 --> 01:65:29,560
three 1-6th of the time.

138
01:65:29,560 --> 01:65:31,280
That's the frequency in the past.

139
01:65:31,280 --> 01:65:33,680
And so there's a 1-6th chance that if we throw it again,

140
01:65:33,680 --> 01:65:34,480
it will be a three.

141
01:65:37,880 --> 01:65:40,840
So virtually no one says there's any philosophical problem

142
01:65:40,840 --> 01:65:42,760
with this concept.

143
01:65:42,760 --> 01:65:44,200
Anyone here?

144
01:65:44,200 --> 01:65:46,480
No, you can't talk about what happened in the past.

145
01:65:46,480 --> 01:65:48,200
Assumes there is a past.

146
01:65:48,200 --> 01:65:50,880
Assumes that something happens.

147
01:65:50,880 --> 01:65:52,680
There are weird philosophical views

148
01:65:52,680 --> 01:65:55,360
where there's someone who rejects everything.

149
01:65:55,360 --> 01:65:59,520
So you can't please everybody.

150
01:65:59,520 --> 01:66:01,600
Now, objective probability theory, unconversial,

151
01:66:01,600 --> 01:66:05,480
but it's not very satisfying when you think about it.

152
01:66:05,480 --> 01:66:07,240
For one thing, objective probability theory

153
01:66:07,240 --> 01:66:09,440
implies that you cannot talk about the probability

154
01:66:09,440 --> 01:66:11,560
of any unique event.

155
01:66:11,560 --> 01:66:14,520
Because how often do you observe a unique event?

156
01:66:14,520 --> 01:66:16,220
For any unique event you're talking about

157
01:66:16,220 --> 01:66:18,720
hasn't happened yet, how many times have you observed it?

158
01:66:18,720 --> 01:66:19,320
Zero.

159
01:66:19,320 --> 01:66:20,000
Zero.

160
01:66:20,000 --> 01:66:23,320
How many times does it come out any particular way?

161
01:66:23,320 --> 01:66:24,360
Zero.

162
01:66:24,360 --> 01:66:27,560
What's zero over zero?

163
01:66:27,560 --> 01:66:28,560
Undefined.

164
01:66:28,560 --> 01:66:29,680
Undefined.

165
01:66:29,680 --> 01:66:30,720
So it's indeterminate.

166
01:66:30,720 --> 01:66:32,960
I think that additional is indeterminate.

167
01:66:32,960 --> 01:66:34,480
It's an indeterminate thing.

168
01:66:34,480 --> 01:66:36,440
Like remember taking limits, L'Hospital's rule,

169
01:66:36,440 --> 01:66:37,240
that kind of thing.

170
01:66:37,240 --> 01:66:41,520
Zero over zero could actually be 1 third.

171
01:66:41,520 --> 01:66:43,040
But anyway, so that's the problem.

172
01:66:43,040 --> 01:66:45,080
So it says you can't talk about the probability

173
01:66:45,080 --> 01:66:47,400
of any unique event.

174
01:66:47,400 --> 01:66:49,320
And that seems weird because don't we often

175
01:66:49,320 --> 01:66:51,160
talk about the probability of unique events?

176
01:66:51,160 --> 01:66:53,560
What's the probability that you will get a job

177
01:66:53,560 --> 01:66:57,280
at Northwestern University?

178
01:66:57,280 --> 01:66:58,600
That's a unique event.

179
01:66:58,600 --> 01:67:00,400
So we're not talking about anybody else,

180
01:67:00,400 --> 01:67:03,600
we're talking about you, this particular case.

181
01:67:03,600 --> 01:67:05,000
And then here's a bigger problem.

182
01:67:05,000 --> 01:67:07,420
You say, well, all right, fine, we lose the unique events,

183
01:67:07,420 --> 01:67:09,440
but how many events are unique?

184
01:67:09,440 --> 01:67:12,920
Strictly speaking, all events are unique.

185
01:67:12,920 --> 01:67:17,240
Even playing roulette is unique because there's only one time

186
01:67:17,240 --> 01:67:20,120
that you're there in that casino,

187
01:67:20,120 --> 01:67:24,040
on that night, at that time, at that table, playing there,

188
01:67:24,040 --> 01:67:26,040
and you don't know that table's fair, do you?

189
01:67:28,480 --> 01:67:32,280
So have you run the test on whether the table's fair?

190
01:67:32,280 --> 01:67:35,200
So in that case, once again, like Ulysses,

191
01:67:35,200 --> 01:67:36,640
unique probability.

192
01:67:36,640 --> 01:67:40,360
Then we get lucky number 33, spinning this wheel.

193
01:67:40,360 --> 01:67:44,040
Me, Brian Kaplan, spinning it here at Harris Casino

194
01:67:44,040 --> 01:67:48,600
at 11.32 PM this night.

195
01:67:48,600 --> 01:67:49,520
That's a unique event.

196
01:67:49,520 --> 01:67:52,360
It's never happened before, it will never happen again.

197
01:67:52,360 --> 01:67:53,860
And if you think about it, any event

198
01:67:53,860 --> 01:67:55,360
can be described as a unique event

199
01:67:55,360 --> 01:67:58,640
because every event is, in fact, a unique event.

200
01:67:58,640 --> 01:68:02,800
An actuary may say, you are a 46-year-old male with a PhD

201
01:68:02,800 --> 01:68:05,520
living in the 22124 zip code.

202
01:68:05,520 --> 01:68:07,440
That's what you are.

203
01:68:07,440 --> 01:68:09,480
I say, well, I'm also, like, yes,

204
01:68:09,480 --> 01:68:11,520
but what about the table for people

205
01:68:11,520 --> 01:68:13,120
who play role-playing games?

206
01:68:13,120 --> 01:68:15,760
What about the table for people who also own a pool table?

207
01:68:15,760 --> 01:68:18,480
What about, like, well, there isn't anyone else in that

208
01:68:18,480 --> 01:68:20,240
who fits that description.

209
01:68:20,240 --> 01:68:24,240
So you can't say, well, my odds are dying.

210
01:68:24,240 --> 01:68:27,240
So, and I'm like, oh, yes, we can.

211
01:68:27,240 --> 01:68:28,800
We're going to do it anyway.

212
01:68:28,800 --> 01:68:30,240
I don't care what philosophers say.

213
01:68:30,240 --> 01:68:33,040
We're going to give up probability in any case.

214
01:68:33,040 --> 01:68:34,080
All right.

215
01:68:34,080 --> 01:68:37,040
Now, when I had this argument again with Walter Bloch

216
01:68:37,040 --> 01:68:39,800
and pointed out that since everything is unique,

217
01:68:39,800 --> 01:68:41,680
he said, well, object to probability is fine.

218
01:68:41,680 --> 01:68:43,800
I said, yeah, but in the real world, everything's unique.

219
01:68:43,800 --> 01:68:45,200
And then he said, great.

220
01:68:45,200 --> 01:68:46,240
Then we can never use it.

221
01:68:46,240 --> 01:68:47,920
Even better.

222
01:68:47,920 --> 01:68:50,080
We can never talk about the probability of anything.

223
01:68:50,080 --> 01:68:51,240
Oh, wonderful.

224
01:68:51,240 --> 01:68:52,080
All right.

225
01:68:52,080 --> 01:68:56,120
Now, you know, even he does say things

226
01:68:56,120 --> 01:68:58,960
that sure sound like probability statements on occasion.

227
01:68:58,960 --> 01:69:01,240
And he has a whole world view where, yeah,

228
01:69:01,240 --> 01:69:02,760
but I'm not speaking as a scientist.

229
01:69:02,760 --> 01:69:05,120
I'm speaking as a person.

230
01:69:05,120 --> 01:69:07,720
Well, is the person wrong?

231
01:69:07,720 --> 01:69:10,200
And so if the person's wrong, then stop saying it.

232
01:69:10,200 --> 01:69:12,160
If the person's right, why not go

233
01:69:12,160 --> 01:69:14,280
and use the language that allows us to communicate

234
01:69:14,280 --> 01:69:16,280
with other people?

235
01:69:16,280 --> 01:69:17,680
I'm good with that.

236
01:69:17,680 --> 01:69:20,120
I don't know.

237
01:69:20,120 --> 01:69:23,920
I cannot successfully channel him on this either.

238
01:69:23,920 --> 01:69:27,040
So once you realize that object to probability theory

239
01:69:27,040 --> 01:69:29,880
leaves us hollow, leaves us without answers

240
01:69:29,880 --> 01:69:32,120
for so many questions that we need to answer,

241
01:69:32,120 --> 01:69:34,640
brings us readily to subject to probability theory.

242
01:69:37,960 --> 01:69:38,960
All right.

243
01:69:38,960 --> 01:69:41,560
So subject to probability theory says

244
01:69:41,560 --> 01:69:45,040
that a subject to probability is simply a degree of belief.

245
01:69:45,040 --> 01:69:46,920
It is a degree of belief that a person

246
01:69:46,920 --> 01:69:48,760
assigns to a proposition.

247
01:69:48,760 --> 01:69:49,960
And that's it.

248
01:69:49,960 --> 01:69:53,920
So you can have a 10% probability, or a 3%, or 99.

249
01:69:53,920 --> 01:69:56,560
So all we need is that probability

250
01:69:56,560 --> 01:69:59,560
has to range from, has to be greater than or equal to 0%,

251
01:69:59,560 --> 01:70:02,080
less than or equal to 100%, or greater

252
01:70:02,080 --> 01:70:06,480
than or equal to 1%, less than or equal to 1%,

253
01:70:06,480 --> 01:70:11,640
less than or equal to 1%, less than or equal to 0%,

254
01:70:11,640 --> 01:70:13,960
greater than or equal to 0%, less than or equal to 1%.

255
01:70:13,960 --> 01:70:14,840
Yes, that's correct.

256
01:70:14,840 --> 01:70:15,880
That's seven other words.

257
01:70:15,880 --> 01:70:16,380
Zero.

258
01:70:19,520 --> 01:70:21,560
Ignore all the words I said and that.

259
01:70:21,560 --> 01:70:23,080
That's good.

260
01:70:23,080 --> 01:70:24,040
OK.

261
01:70:24,040 --> 01:70:26,920
And normally we say that a probability of 0

262
01:70:26,920 --> 01:70:28,320
means impossible.

263
01:70:28,320 --> 01:70:31,520
Were there any undergraduate math majors here?

264
01:70:31,520 --> 01:70:32,020
Good.

265
01:70:32,020 --> 01:70:34,840
OK, in that case, it's true.

266
01:70:34,840 --> 01:70:37,960
So if you remember infinitesimal things and so on,

267
01:70:37,960 --> 01:70:40,000
technically speaking, it is logically

268
01:70:40,000 --> 01:70:41,960
possible for a zero probability event to occur.

269
01:70:41,960 --> 01:70:44,240
Like, don't worry about it for all practical purposes.

270
01:70:44,240 --> 01:70:46,120
That probability of 0 is possible.

271
01:70:46,120 --> 01:70:49,400
And similarly, probability of 1 is certain.

272
01:70:49,400 --> 01:70:52,720
And then the other thing is that since something always happens,

273
01:70:52,720 --> 01:70:56,320
have you ever been in a case where nothing happened?

274
01:70:56,320 --> 01:70:59,960
It's like, even nothing is something.

275
01:70:59,960 --> 01:71:02,840
So then it's also that since it is certain that something

276
01:71:02,840 --> 01:71:07,120
or other will occur, then the summation of all probabilities

277
01:71:07,120 --> 01:71:08,200
must equal 1.

278
01:71:08,200 --> 01:71:10,440
As long as these probabilities are mutually exclusive

279
01:71:10,440 --> 01:71:13,800
and jointly exhaustive, then the probability of everything

280
01:71:13,800 --> 01:71:17,880
will sum up that something or other happening has its 1.

281
01:71:17,880 --> 01:71:19,880
And of course, this could be probability

282
01:71:19,880 --> 01:71:22,080
that I answer the phone, or my son answers the phone,

283
01:71:22,080 --> 01:71:23,440
or my daughter answers the phone,

284
01:71:23,440 --> 01:71:25,560
or somebody else answers the phone.

285
01:71:25,560 --> 01:71:27,240
And if you do it that way, then that's

286
01:71:27,240 --> 01:71:28,860
mutually exclusive, jointly exhaustive.

287
01:71:28,860 --> 01:71:30,400
So that must sum to 1.

288
01:71:30,400 --> 01:71:32,480
Because actually, no one has to answer the phone.

289
01:71:32,480 --> 01:71:34,480
So last one would be or nobody answers the phone.

290
01:71:34,480 --> 01:71:35,560
And that will sum to 1.

291
01:71:35,560 --> 01:71:37,240
Conditional on the phone being answered,

292
01:71:37,240 --> 01:71:38,280
then someone answered it.

293
01:71:41,200 --> 01:71:43,400
Now, why do people have a problem

294
01:71:43,400 --> 01:71:45,400
with subjective probability?

295
01:71:45,400 --> 01:71:47,200
The main argument against it is just

296
01:71:47,200 --> 01:71:48,880
that it's unrealistic to say that people

297
01:71:48,880 --> 01:71:50,800
have subjective probabilities.

298
01:71:50,800 --> 01:71:53,120
Because normally, when beings, as opposed

299
01:71:53,120 --> 01:71:56,320
to Vulcans and robots, do not assign probabilities to events.

300
01:71:56,320 --> 01:72:00,760
So most people do not say, there is a 0.3723 probability

301
01:72:00,760 --> 01:72:03,840
that the next meal is a hamburger, something like that.

302
01:72:03,840 --> 01:72:04,840
Never tell me the odds.

303
01:72:04,840 --> 01:72:06,400
So that's the human way of speaking.

304
01:72:06,400 --> 01:72:09,680
So in science fiction, usually it

305
01:72:09,680 --> 01:72:13,760
will be robots or Vulcans or other inhuman-type creatures

306
01:72:13,760 --> 01:72:18,040
that will be very inclined to give precise probabilities.

307
01:72:18,040 --> 01:72:21,640
And then what does a truly human human like Han Solo

308
01:72:21,640 --> 01:72:23,920
say when you start talking this way?

309
01:72:23,920 --> 01:72:25,000
Never tell me the odds.

310
01:72:25,000 --> 01:72:26,560
Never tell me the odds.

311
01:72:26,560 --> 01:72:29,760
Believe it or not, there are odds.

312
01:72:29,760 --> 01:72:31,480
Science doesn't want to hear them.

313
01:72:31,480 --> 01:72:33,680
But there are people who say, never tell me the odds,

314
01:72:33,680 --> 01:72:36,240
because you can't talk about odds.

315
01:72:36,240 --> 01:72:41,080
So I guess it's unrealistic to say it.

316
01:72:41,080 --> 01:72:45,120
Now, my reply to this is that while it

317
01:72:45,120 --> 01:72:49,520
is true that most people don't go around putting

318
01:72:49,520 --> 01:72:52,600
little stickers with numbers on every single event,

319
01:72:52,600 --> 01:72:55,240
nevertheless, people almost always

320
01:72:55,240 --> 01:72:57,680
have some probabilities in the back of their minds.

321
01:72:57,680 --> 01:72:58,520
So think about this.

322
01:72:58,520 --> 01:73:00,520
Probabilities like willingness to pay.

323
01:73:00,520 --> 01:73:02,600
How many people go around and tell you

324
01:73:02,600 --> 01:73:06,400
what their willingness to pay for everything is?

325
01:73:06,400 --> 01:73:08,800
My willingness to pay for a new mailbox

326
01:73:08,800 --> 01:73:11,360
would be $17.33.

327
01:73:11,360 --> 01:73:15,840
Willingness to pay for this mailbox to be of the green

328
01:73:15,840 --> 01:73:19,720
huge would be an upcharge of $1.33.

329
01:73:19,720 --> 01:73:20,220
Right?

330
01:73:20,220 --> 01:73:21,800
Again, this is unusual behavior.

331
01:73:21,800 --> 01:73:23,500
And yet, I don't know anyone in economics

332
01:73:23,500 --> 01:73:26,120
who has a problem with the constant willingness to pay.

333
01:73:26,120 --> 01:73:29,520
Because, yes, while people may not, generally,

334
01:73:29,520 --> 01:73:31,720
do not have a giant book listing their willingness

335
01:73:31,720 --> 01:73:33,760
to pay for everything, nevertheless,

336
01:73:33,760 --> 01:73:37,200
there's some willingness to pay in the back of your minds.

337
01:73:37,200 --> 01:73:38,920
It is a meaningful question to say, yeah,

338
01:73:38,920 --> 01:73:41,040
but how much would you be willing to pay for this?

339
01:73:41,040 --> 01:73:42,680
Similarly, it is a meaningful question

340
01:73:42,680 --> 01:73:46,280
to say, how likely do you think this is?

341
01:73:46,280 --> 01:73:47,400
It doesn't seem like that.

342
01:73:47,400 --> 01:73:50,880
So it seems 50% likely, 75%, 32% likely.

343
01:73:50,880 --> 01:73:54,320
These are all reasonable questions to ask someone.

344
01:73:54,320 --> 01:73:56,160
So yeah, so if you think about probabilities

345
01:73:56,160 --> 01:73:57,760
being like willingness to pay, I'll

346
01:73:57,760 --> 01:73:59,800
say either you've got to get rid of both of them.

347
01:73:59,800 --> 01:74:02,000
I don't think even Walter Block wants to do that.

348
01:74:02,000 --> 01:74:03,240
Or you've got to accept both.

349
01:74:03,240 --> 01:74:04,660
So yeah, well, they're both things

350
01:74:04,660 --> 01:74:07,320
where economics does get us to be more explicit about it.

351
01:74:07,320 --> 01:74:09,360
But yeah, if you do hang around with economists,

352
01:74:09,360 --> 01:74:12,080
you do get more questions like, what is your willingness to pay?

353
01:74:12,080 --> 01:74:14,320
So I know one economist who's really

354
01:74:14,320 --> 01:74:16,720
worried that he's going to get protested one day.

355
01:74:16,720 --> 01:74:18,680
And he talks about it all the time.

356
01:74:18,680 --> 01:74:20,840
And I'm like, I'm so tired of hearing that.

357
01:74:20,840 --> 01:74:23,440
What is your willingness to pay for an insurance policy that

358
01:74:23,440 --> 01:74:26,320
absolutely guarantees that you will never be protested ever

359
01:74:26,320 --> 01:74:27,600
in your entire life?

360
01:74:27,600 --> 01:74:28,880
He said $100,000.

361
01:74:28,880 --> 01:74:32,160
I'm like, man, you're really worried.

362
01:74:35,160 --> 01:74:36,600
It's not a reasonable number.

363
01:74:36,600 --> 01:74:38,000
I'd like to see you assume that.

364
01:74:38,000 --> 01:74:42,440
I don't know, $500 maybe for a lifetime insurance policy.

365
01:74:42,440 --> 01:74:43,480
Just look so far.

366
01:74:43,480 --> 01:74:44,800
I mean, what's been cool to me?

367
01:74:44,800 --> 01:74:46,960
I don't see why this would stop.

368
01:74:46,960 --> 01:74:51,520
And I think you're just getting agitated by scary stories

369
01:74:51,520 --> 01:74:53,440
and forgetting that what is the news?

370
01:74:53,440 --> 01:74:54,760
It's an inventory of the worst thing that

371
01:74:54,760 --> 01:74:55,920
happened on Earth every day.

372
01:74:55,920 --> 01:74:58,600
So you shouldn't basically just pay on that.

373
01:74:58,600 --> 01:75:02,000
Anyway, so that is a way that economists think,

374
01:75:02,000 --> 01:75:03,360
which is helpful.

375
01:75:03,360 --> 01:75:06,760
The elite least you had to say, $100,000.

376
01:75:06,760 --> 01:75:09,080
So all right, I guess if I were that worried,

377
01:75:09,080 --> 01:75:11,200
I might talk about it a lot too.

378
01:75:11,200 --> 01:75:13,240
Although when my house lost more than that money,

379
01:75:13,240 --> 01:75:16,600
I didn't think I needed to keep talking about it.

380
01:75:16,600 --> 01:75:19,560
So maybe you could play a bunch of fights, but you're done.

381
01:75:19,560 --> 01:75:21,480
So I'm not going to protest here.

382
01:75:21,480 --> 01:75:24,880
Anyway, all right.

383
01:75:24,880 --> 01:75:28,320
Now, further objection to subjective probabilities

384
01:75:28,320 --> 01:75:30,920
is that if you actually ask someone,

385
01:75:30,920 --> 01:75:33,160
what is the probability you're saying to this?

386
01:75:33,160 --> 01:75:38,040
A lot of people will say, I just don't know.

387
01:75:38,040 --> 01:75:42,120
People will stonewall you and not give you a probability.

388
01:75:42,120 --> 01:75:45,880
In fact, you may be treated as being rude for actually asking

389
01:75:45,880 --> 01:75:47,000
probabilities.

390
01:75:47,000 --> 01:75:49,000
Someone says, I'm really worried about terrorism.

391
01:75:49,000 --> 01:75:50,400
What's the probability you think?

392
01:75:50,400 --> 01:75:52,000
I don't know.

393
01:75:52,000 --> 01:75:53,800
I don't know about it, but I shouldn't even

394
01:75:53,800 --> 01:75:55,480
have to think about questions like that.

395
01:75:58,360 --> 01:75:59,520
Now, here's the key thing.

396
01:75:59,520 --> 01:76:01,040
What if you have to guess?

397
01:76:01,040 --> 01:76:04,240
And in real life, you do have to guess.

398
01:76:04,240 --> 01:76:06,600
Every time you eat a meal, you are making a guess

399
01:76:06,600 --> 01:76:09,600
that meal is not poisonous.

400
01:76:09,600 --> 01:76:11,360
So far, I assume you've guessed correctly.

401
01:76:14,400 --> 01:76:15,600
Lethal poison.

402
01:76:15,600 --> 01:76:19,360
I guess I've had food poisoning once or twice in my life.

403
01:76:19,360 --> 01:76:22,640
Didn't kill me, obviously, but it's very unpleasant.

404
01:76:22,640 --> 01:76:25,240
If I had guessed correctly on those cases,

405
01:76:25,240 --> 01:76:26,640
I would not have eaten that food.

406
01:76:26,640 --> 01:76:28,360
No way.

407
01:76:28,360 --> 01:76:29,960
The day I was doing my job market talk,

408
01:76:29,960 --> 01:76:34,200
Tyler Cowen tried to get me to try raw Peruvian fish.

409
01:76:34,200 --> 01:76:38,160
I'm not trying raw Peruvian fish on the day of my job talk.

410
01:76:38,160 --> 01:76:38,800
Super slow.

411
01:76:41,360 --> 01:76:43,400
No way.

412
01:76:43,400 --> 01:76:49,840
That was my guess, is that 5% chance I'd be sick,

413
01:76:49,840 --> 01:76:50,760
but that was enough.

414
01:76:50,760 --> 01:76:54,480
I did not want to be sick on the fourth night.

415
01:76:54,480 --> 01:76:56,200
So in real life, you actually do.

416
01:76:56,200 --> 01:76:58,440
You remember when I had my kids, and I always

417
01:76:58,440 --> 01:77:00,600
wanted to say, wow, not only when I cross the street

418
01:77:00,600 --> 01:77:03,840
am I now gambling that I can get across the street alive myself.

419
01:77:03,840 --> 01:77:06,560
I've got about three lives that I can get across the street

420
01:77:06,560 --> 01:77:08,440
safely.

421
01:77:08,440 --> 01:77:11,040
Look to the left, to the right, and left again.

422
01:77:11,040 --> 01:77:13,040
Let's go.

423
01:77:13,040 --> 01:77:15,440
What if there was a car driving 200 miles per hour?

424
01:77:15,440 --> 01:77:17,840
You wouldn't be able to, like, you'd still die.

425
01:77:17,840 --> 01:77:20,320
Yeah, but I've never seen that happen on a city street,

426
01:77:20,320 --> 01:77:23,160
so it seems very unlikely.

427
01:77:23,160 --> 01:77:25,600
Could happen.

428
01:77:25,600 --> 01:77:27,880
Now, a common sophism that is associated

429
01:77:27,880 --> 01:77:30,520
with the objection to subjective probability theory

430
01:77:30,520 --> 01:77:32,840
is say, look, no one can ever know that.

431
01:77:32,840 --> 01:77:35,120
There's just questions that are unanswerable,

432
01:77:35,120 --> 01:77:37,320
that are beyond human comprehension,

433
01:77:37,320 --> 01:77:39,760
that it's arrogant even to have an opinion on.

434
01:77:39,760 --> 01:77:41,160
Like, I remember when I was a kid,

435
01:77:41,160 --> 01:77:42,680
my friend made fun of me for saying,

436
01:77:42,680 --> 01:77:44,760
there have to be exoplanets.

437
01:77:44,760 --> 01:77:47,120
This cannot be the only solar system with planets in it.

438
01:77:47,120 --> 01:77:48,160
There's got to be others.

439
01:77:48,160 --> 01:77:49,240
I bet they're everywhere.

440
01:77:49,240 --> 01:77:51,240
And he said, you can't know that, Brian.

441
01:77:51,240 --> 01:77:53,280
You can't possibly say one way or the other.

442
01:77:53,280 --> 01:77:54,280
Ha.

443
01:77:54,280 --> 01:77:55,000
All right.

444
01:77:55,000 --> 01:77:57,880
And well, what if we had made a bet?

445
01:77:57,880 --> 01:77:59,480
And yes, if we had made the bets,

446
01:77:59,480 --> 01:78:01,120
I would have bet, I think I would have bet a lot on that,

447
01:78:01,120 --> 01:78:03,280
and I would have totally been vindicated by science.

448
01:78:03,280 --> 01:78:06,320
And now there's thousands of identified exoplanets.

449
01:78:06,320 --> 01:78:07,120
Great.

450
01:78:07,120 --> 01:78:09,880
I love reading about exoplanets.

451
01:78:09,880 --> 01:78:11,480
No interest in subatomic particles,

452
01:78:11,480 --> 01:78:14,320
but any planet will always interest me.

453
01:78:14,320 --> 01:78:15,920
Well, that's a place I could colonize.

454
01:78:15,920 --> 01:78:18,200
I could never live on a fork.

455
01:78:18,200 --> 01:78:20,280
But good in what sense?

456
01:78:20,280 --> 01:78:21,800
Don't worry about that.

457
01:78:21,800 --> 01:78:24,680
I can imagine living on the planet.

458
01:78:24,680 --> 01:78:28,720
Anyway, common selfism is to say no one can possibly know x.

459
01:78:28,720 --> 01:78:30,560
There's two ways that we can interpret this.

460
01:78:30,560 --> 01:78:32,560
One is, if you mean no one can know x

461
01:78:32,560 --> 01:78:34,680
with absolute certainty, then that's totally true,

462
01:78:34,680 --> 01:78:36,920
but totally uninteresting.

463
01:78:36,920 --> 01:78:38,400
Yes, I can never know with certainty

464
01:78:38,400 --> 01:78:42,720
that I will not die from eating a bowl of Cheerios.

465
01:78:42,720 --> 01:78:43,840
So what?

466
01:78:43,840 --> 01:78:46,360
I'm still perfectly happy to bet my life on these Cheerios.

467
01:78:46,360 --> 01:78:47,680
They seem fine.

468
01:78:47,680 --> 01:78:49,960
So looks good for me.

469
01:78:49,960 --> 01:78:51,320
OK.

470
01:78:51,320 --> 01:78:53,240
So you mean no one can know it with certainty,

471
01:78:53,240 --> 01:78:55,200
then it's obvious, but uninteresting.

472
01:78:55,200 --> 01:78:56,840
On the other hand, if you mean no one

473
01:78:56,840 --> 01:79:01,040
can know anything at all about x, then it's clearly false.

474
01:79:01,040 --> 01:79:02,920
And how can you know it's so clearly false?

475
01:79:02,920 --> 01:79:03,920
Well, how about this?

476
01:79:03,920 --> 01:79:06,560
If you really think no one can know about it at all,

477
01:79:06,560 --> 01:79:08,040
how about we just make a bet?

478
01:79:08,040 --> 01:79:10,440
So what does that mean?

479
01:79:10,440 --> 01:79:11,560
50-50?

480
01:79:11,560 --> 01:79:15,160
Fine, we'll give you 50-50 on a Martian invasion tomorrow.

481
01:79:15,160 --> 01:79:17,080
I'll give you three to one even.

482
01:79:17,080 --> 01:79:18,560
How about that?

483
01:79:18,560 --> 01:79:24,200
So when you all say, so I don't know if you've heard of that.

484
01:79:24,200 --> 01:79:26,560
So one of my main activities on the internet

485
01:79:26,560 --> 01:79:28,000
is just betting people that I think

486
01:79:28,000 --> 01:79:30,440
are being giant blowhards and who claim

487
01:79:30,440 --> 01:79:32,160
to know way more than they do.

488
01:79:32,160 --> 01:79:32,800
So let's see.

489
01:79:32,800 --> 01:79:35,560
